  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 244 AM EDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nWednesday through Friday: Dry conditions will prevail as ensemble \nguidance depicts the polar jet stream situated across the northern \ntier of the CONUS. In the absence of any fronts or airmass changes, \ntemperatures will moderate -- highs in the upper 70s to mid-80s and \nlows in the 50s.\n\nSaturday and Sunday: After the aforementioned dry period, Gulf \nmoisture is progged to increase from south to north. National \nHurricane Center (NHC) has identified an area of the southwestern \nGulf that could be conducive for tropical development. At this time, \nfor north and central Georgia, the takeaway is that showers and \nstorms will return. Additionally, dense cloud cover (and potentially \nanother cold air damming \"wedge\") could bring cooler temperatures -- \nhighs in the 70s are currently forecasted. It appears that breezy \nconditions could occur as well, especially if the County Warning \nArea (CWA) ends up within the interplay of the wedge and the \nperiphery of the potentially-tropical area of low pressure.",
